  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Storm chances linger through at least Saturday,\nwith a possibly drier finish to the weekend.\n\nThe latest surface analysis places a cold front across southern\nPennsylvania back across southeastern Ohio into the middle of\nKentucky. Within the warm sector, numerous showers have begun to\npress eastward across the region. Although much of this activity\nis on the lighter side, recent radar returns are a bit heftier\nsouth of I-66/U.S. 50. Such elements will bring moderate\nintensity showers to areas south of the Washington, D.C. metro\ndown into southern Maryland. The more regional-scale radar\nimagery shows a bit of a lull behind this wave, but with\nadditional activity back over West Virginia. Most areas see shower\nchances diminish toward midday, but with some additional\nafternoon to evening development over the Potomac Highlands into\nthe central Virginia Piedmont region. Overall conditions today\nwill yield more clouds than sun. This is accompanied by high \ntemperatures in the mid 70s to low 80s, locally a bit cooler \nacross the mountains. \n\nThe cold front initially north of the Mason-Dixon Line is\nforecast to settle toward the Virginia-North Carolina border by\ntonight. Despite the frontal passage, its more diffuse nature\nwill only bring a limited drop in temperatures and humidity\nlevels. Saturday morning will start off in the 60s which comes\nwith further shower and thunderstorm chances throughout the day.\nThe focus is driven by an elongated trough currently crossing\nthe Arklatex states. As this array of embedded shortwaves lift\nacross the Tennessee Valley toward the southern Mid-Atlantic,\nstorm chances increase in earnest. The 00Z high-resolution model\nsuite shows an uptick in rainfall amounts, some of which are on\nthe higher end (1 to 2 inches in 24 hours). With precipitable\nwater values in the 1.75 to 2.00 inch range and deep warm cloud\nlayers, some fairly efficient storms are possible in this\nenvironment. As such, the Weather Prediction Center supports a\nSlight risk for excessive rainfall on Saturday, especially\nacross the I-95 urban corridor. Will continue to monitor the\nsituation as new mesoscale guidance arrives.\n\nComing off temperatures similar to today, expect a slight warm\nup into Sunday with highs returning to the mid 80s. Some\nresidual shower activity is possible to start the day. However,\nthe local area will be in the wake of the passing shortwave\ntrough. Expect some increase in humidity as the boundary to the\nsouth returns northward as a warm front. Unlike previous days, a\nfair amount of sunshine is possible, particularly for the second\nhalf of the day. Sunday night's low temperatures will fall into\nthe 60s to low 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm ahead of a mid-week cold front.\n\nH5 ridging builds Mon through Wed, before weakening as sw trofs\nand associated sfc cold front move through midweek. Some spread\non exactly what day this happens, but overall consensus around \nThu. This could bring showers and thunderstorms, but probability\nof svr wx remains low.",
